<title>ISFDB FAQ: How is the ISFDB organized?</title>
</head>
<body bgcolor="#ffffff" text="#000000" >

<h2><a name="3">How is the ISFDB organized?</a></h2>
Just a couple of comments before we get rolling here. First off, it
should be made clear that, from a computer science point-of-view,
this isn't <i>really</i> a database - it's a collection of lists with some
list-processing software; there's no rocket science going on here.
That said, nothing precludes us from building a <i>real</i> database
system. The problem at present is that no tools exist for data entry,
or to conduct database merges. The file formats listed below reflect
this fact, allowing for data entry via text editors in a format
which minimizes errors.
<p>
The ISFDB can be broken down into several subcomponents: the raw database files,
where data is entered; the database compilers, which compile the raw database files
into a format usable by the database engine; the compiled database files; and the
executable binaries that make up the ISFDB engine.

<h2><a name="3A">Raw Database Files</a></h2>
The raw database files are located in the <i>dbase.ascii</i> directory, and
come in four flavors: <i>titles</i>, <i>authors</i>, <i>publications</i>, 
and <i>awards</i>. The various database files all use the same basic format 
for an entry:
<pre>
    object_name {
        attributes
    }
</pre>
Where an attribute constists of a two-letter attribute code name followed by
an assigned attribute value:
<pre>
    AC=|Attribute Value|
</pre>
An actual example of a short fiction title entry:
<pre>
  Even the Queen {
    AE=|Connie Willis|
    YR=|1992|
    SL=|ss|
    PB=|ASFAPR92,TYBSF10,NEAW1994A,NEAW1994B|
  }
</pre>
Each object can have an arbitrary number of attributes, but the database
engine only recognizes a predefined set of attributes. Unrecognized attribute
types are ignored. The recognized two-letter attribute codes recognized by
each database file type are:

<h3><a name="3A1">Title Entries</a></h3>
  There are currently nine database files that
  use the title format: <i>NOVELS</i>, <i>SHORTFICTION</i>, <i>COLLECTIONS</i>,
  <i>ANTHOLOGIES</i>, <i>NONFICTION</i>, <i>NONGENRE</i>, <i>POEMS</i>, 
  <i>ARTWORK</i>, and <i>ESSAYS</i>. The recognized attribute codes for the title 
  format are:
<p>
<ol>
<li>AE = Author/Editor
<li>YR = Year of first publication, or copyright date; whichever is more accurate.
<li>SL = Story length. Originally intended to categorize short fiction titles, this
	 field is now overloaded in other files as well. Since the field goes unused
	 in the other files, it's an easy way to add some functionality without extending
	 the database format. The various forms of use are:
	 <ol>
	   <li>SHORTFICTION - This field allows the contents of a magazine to be
	   output in a manner similar to a magazine table of contents page.  The 
	   attribute value should be one of: <i>nv</i> (novella), <i>nt</i> (novelette), 
	   or <i>ss</i> (short story). 
	   <li> ANTHOLOGIES, COLLECTIONS, NONGENRE, NOVELS - The field can be 
	   utilized to indicate novelizations, which are tagged via <i>nvz</i>,
	   and to indicate juveniles via <i>jvn</i>.
	   <li> OMNIBUS - The field can be used to summarize the type of contents.
	   <i>/2N</i> indicates that the omnibus contains 2 novels, while <i>/2C</i>
	   indicates that it contains 2 collections.
	   <li> SERIALS - An attempt is made to summarize a novel's serialized publications
	   below the novel. This is normally done by comparing the portion of the title
	   prior to the last left parenthesis in the title of the serial to the title of the
	   novel. However, Serials are often published under different titles than a later
	   novel printing. For this reason, the SL field can be used in the serial entry
	   to indicate what the title of the later novel is.
	</ol>
<li>PB = Publication list. Consists of a list of comma-separated publication
	 tags. These tags should be entered in chronological order. Publication
	 tags are explained below under the publication entry section.
</ol>
<p>

<h3><a name="3A2">Author Entries</a></h3>
  There is one database file that uses the
  author format: <i>AUTHORS</i>. The object name for this format is the
  accepted working name of the author. The recognized attribute codes for 
  the author format are:
<p>
<ol>
<li>LN = Legal Name of the author. Should be in Lastname, Firstname format.
<li>BP = Birthplace. Also used to denote change in citizenship, i.e.:
	 BP=|USSR;USA|. 
<li>BD = Year of Birth Date.
<li>DD = Year of Date Date.
<li>PS = List of known pseudonyms. By convention, each pseudonym is separated
	 by a semicolon.
<li>NT = Biographical information.
</ol>
<p>
  An example author entry:
<pre>
  Cordwainer Smith {
    LN=|Linebarger, Paul Myron Anthony|
    BP=|USA|
    BD=|1913|
    DD=|1966|
    PS=|Karloman Jungahr; Carmichael Smith|
  }
</pre>

<h3><a name="3A3">Publication Entries</a></h3>
  There are currently three database files 
  that use the publication format: <i>BOOKS</i>, <i>ZINES</i>, and <i>ASIMOVS</i>.
  Once a zine collects enough data, it should be broken out of the <i>ZINES</i>
  file into a separate file for easy maintenance.  The object name is the 
  published title as it appeared on the publication. The recognized attribute 
  codes for the publication format are:
<p>
<ol>
<li>AB = Abbreviation tag. This is a unique, short tag used to refer to the
	 publication. Current convention for constructing the tag is to take
	 the first letter of each word in the title, and append the year. If
	 there are less than four words, then take four arbitrary but sensible
	 letters from the title. If the tag still collides with a previously
	 declared tag, append an "A", or "B", or whatever. The tags just need
	 to be unique.
<li>AE = Author/Editor.
<li>YR = Year of Publication.
<li>IS = ISBN.
<li>PB = Publisher.
<li>PP = Pages.
<li>TP = Type. Should be one of <i>hc</i> (hardcover), <i>pb</i> (paperback),
         <i>tp</i> (trade paperback).
<li>CV = Cover Artist.
<li>NT = Note. Can be used to indicate interesting data, such as limited editions,
	 SFBC editions, or whatever.
</ol>
<p>
  An example publication entry:
<pre>
  The Uplift War {
      AB=|TUWA1987B|
      YR=|1987|
      PB=|Phantasia Press|
      IS=|0-932-09644-1|
      TP=|hc|
      PR=|22.00|
      PP=|506|
      CV=|Wayne Barlowe|
      NT=|Limited edition of 3475 signed copies|
  }
</pre>

<h3><a name="3A4">Awards Entries</a></h3>
  There are currently eight database files 
  that use the awards format: <i>CAMPBELL</i>, <i>CLARKE</i>, <i>HUGOS</i>,
  <i>LOCUS</i>, <i>NEBULAS</i>, <i>PKDICK</i>, <i>STOKER</i>, <i>AURORA</i>, and <i>WFA</i>.
  The object name for the entry should be the title of the work nominated
  for the award.  The recognized attribute codes for the awards format are:
<p>
<ol>
<li>AE = Author/Editor.
<li>YR = Year of award. Note that this is the year selected by the
	 awards committee. It is not necessarily the year of publication,
	 nor the year the award is given out. Check your local award
	 listings.
<li>TP = Type of award. This is the award category. Categories currently
	 supported by the database engine are:
<pre>
	 <i>n</i>    [Novel]
	 <i>lf</i>   [Long-Form Fiction]
	 <i>nsf</i>  [Locus - Novel, Science Fiction]
	 <i>nft</i>  [Locus - Novel, Fantasy]
	 <i>nho</i>  [Locus - Novel, Dark Fantasy/Horror]
	 <i>fn</i>   [First Novel]
	 <i>nv</i>   [Novella]
	 <i>nt</i>   [Novelette]
	 <i>ss</i>   [Short Story]
	 <i>sf</i>   [Short Fiction]
	 <i>c</i>    [Collection]
	 <i>a</i>    [Anthology]
	 <i>om</i>   [Other Media]
	 <i>nf</i>   [Non-Fiction]
	 <i>ac</i>   [Anthology/Collection]
	 <i>lfa</i>  [Lifetime Achievement]
	 <i>lfac</i> [Lifetime Contribution]
	 <i>at</i>   [Artist]
	 <i>sn</i>   [Special Award (Non-Professional)]
	 <i>sp</i>   [Special Award (Professional)]
	 <i>dp</i>   [Dramatic Presentation]
	 <i>ed</i>   [Professional Editor]
	 <i>fz</i>   [Fanzine]
	 <i>fw</i>   [Fan Writer]
	 <i>fa</i>   [Fan Artist]
	 <i>jc</i>   [John W. Campbell, Jr. Award (Best New Writer)]
</pre>
<li>LV = Level of award. The attribute value should be a single integer.
    A level of "1" indicates an award win; a level of "2" indicates an
    award nomination; "3" and above are used by the Locus Poll to indicate
    the title's place on the poll. There is currently no notation for
    Honorable Mention.
</ol>
<p>
  An example awards entry from the LOCUS database:
<pre>
  Hyperion {
      AE=|Dan Simmons|
      YR=|1990|
      TP=|nsf|
      LV=|1|
  }
</pre>

<h3><a name="3A6">Biographic Entries</a></h3>
The biographic entries differ in style from the other database files. 
The format is basically:
<pre>
    Author=|Biographic data|
</pre>
The data is completely free-form, with no restrictions other than it may 
not contain the '|' character. There is currently a length restriction of 
8192 bytes on this area, but we can always increase it.

<h3><a name="3A5">Entry Conventions</a></h3>
Special notations are available to indicate collaborations, variant titles,
and pseudonyms.
<ol>
<li>Collaborations. The '+' operator can be used to signify a collaboration.
For instance, the AE attribute for <i>Footfall</i> is:
<pre>
    AE=|Larry Niven+Jerry Pournelle|
</pre>
<li>Pseudonyms. The '^' operator can be used to signify a work 
published under a pseudonym. 
For instance, the AE attribute for <i>The Running Man</i>
is:
<pre>
    AE=|Stephen King^Richard Bachman|
</pre>
<li>Complex Author Constructions. The '^' and '+' operators can be used
with parenthesis to indicate more complex author situations. For instance,
the joint pseudonym of Lewis Padgett, as used by C. L. Moore and Henry
Kuttner, would be denoted as:
<pre>
    AE=|(C. L. Moore+Henry Kuttner)^Lewis Padgett|
</pre>
While the collaboration of a pseudonym with a nonpseudonym on 
<i>Dark Conception</i> would be denoted as:
<pre>
    AE=|(Alexei Panshin^Louis J. A. Adams)+Joe L. Hensley|
</pre>
Self-collaborations, as on <i>Solo</i> would be denoted as:
<pre>
    AE=|Steve Perry+(Steve Perry^Jesse Peel)|
</pre>
<li>Variant Titles. Again, the '^' operator can be used to denote variant
titles. Each title may have an unlimited number of variant titles. For 
instance, the entry for Niven and Pournelle's <i>The Gripping Hand</i> is:
<pre>
    The Gripping Hand^The Moat Around Murcheson's Eye (UK)
</pre>
Variant titles are denoted in a bibliography as <i>[vt Variant Title]</i>.
For instance, the bibliography for Ian McDonald shows:
<pre>
  Terminal Cafe
    [vt Necroville (UK)] (1994)[<b>Lc1995 n</b>]
</pre>
<p>
<li>Cover Artists. The CV attribute is used to designate the cover artists.
Unlike the fiction data, the artwork entries are machine-generated from
the CV data, so it needs to be in a specific format. The simpliest format
is simply placing the artist's name in the attribute value area:
<pre>
    CV=|Michael Whelan|
</pre>
If the artwork was a magazine cover created for a specific work of fiction,
the entry should be of the form CV=|<i>artist</i> <b>for</b> "<i>title</i>"|:
<pre>
    CV=|Wojtek Siudmak for "Forgiveness Day"|
</pre>
<hr>

<h2><a name="3B">Database Compilers</a></h2>
There are six database compilers, one for each database type:
<i>cctitles</i>,
<i>ccauthors</i>,
<i>ccawards</i>,
<i>ccpubs</i>,
<i>ccinterv</i>, and
<i>ccreviews</i>,
The order in which the compilers are run is important, since some
dependencies exist on certain database files. For this reason, the
script <i>ccdbase</i> is provided which runs the compilers in the 
correct order and places the output in the expected directories.
<i>ccdbase</i> should be run be executing a <i>make dbase</i> command
from the top-level Makefile.

<ol>
<li><a name="3B1">cctitles</a>.
<i>cctitles</i> takes either a <i>-t</i> or <i>-y</i> option. The <i>-t</i>
option creates the title database from those files mentioned above which
use the title format. The output of entries is in the same order as they appear
in the raw database files, indexed by title. The <i>ccdbase</i> script sorts 
the title dbase before moving on to the next compile phase. This is 
accomplished with the sort command <i>booksort</i>, which performs a book-oriented
sort on the database by ignoring leading <i>A</i>'s, <i>An</i>'s, or <i>The</i>'s.
<i>ccdbase</i> installs the final title database in <i>dbase.compiled/titles.dbase</i>.
<p>
The <i>-y</i> option creates an author-indexed version of the same database. This
is used to speed the generation of a specific author's bibliography.
<i>ccdbase</i> installs the author index in <i>dbase.compiled/titles.xba</i>.
<p>
<li><a name="3B2">ccauthors</a>.
<i>ccauthors</i> compiles the author information into a file installed by
<i>ccdbase</i> at <i>dbase.compiled/authors.dbase</i>.
<p>
<li><a name="3B3">ccpubs</a>.
<i>ccpubs</i> takes either a <i>-a</i> or <i>-p</i> option. The <i>-a</i>
option creates the ARTWORK database file from the CV attributes. The <i>-p</i>
option compiles the publication information into a file installed by
<i>ccdbase</i> at <i>dbase.compiled/pubs.dbase</i>.
<p>
<li><a name="3B4">ccawards</a>.
<i>ccawards</i> takes either a <i>-t</i>, <i>-a</i>, or <i>-y</i> options.
The <i>-t</i> option creates the awards database from those files mentioned above which
use the awards format.  <i>ccdbase</i> installs the final awards database in 
<i>dbase.compiled/awards.dbase</i>.
<p>
The <i>-y</i> option creates a year-indexed version of the same database. This
is used to speed the generation of a specific award year listing.
<i>ccdbase</i> installs the year index in <i>dbase.compiled/awards.xby</i>.
<p>
The <i>-a</i> option creates an author-indexed version of the same database. This
is used to speed the generation of the authors bibliography.
<i>ccdbase</i> installs the author index in <i>dbase.compiled/awards.xba</i>.
<p>
<li><a name="3B5">ccinterv</a>.
<i>ccinterv</i> compiles interview information and auto-generates the file
<i>TITLES.INTERV</i>. The first stream of output is used to construct the
<i>interviews.dbase</i> file, which links interviews to authors and publications. The
second stream is the <i>TITLES.INTERV</i> file, while links interviews to those
conducting the interview.
<p>
<li><a name="3B6">ccreviews</a>.
<i>ccreviews</i> compiles review information and auto-generates the file
<i>TITLES.REVIEWS</i>. The first stream of output is used to construct the
<i>reviews.dbase</i> file, which links reviews to authors/titles and publications. The
second stream is the <i>TITLES.REVIEWS</i> file, while links reviews to those
conducting the reviews.
</ol>
<hr>

<h2><a name="3C">Compiled Database Files</a></h2>
As described above, there is one database file for each of the
four database types. Additionally, there are three cross-indexed
files for improved performance. The format of these files is currently
ascii-oriented, but may change to a binary format for efficiency as 
the database grows in size. The internal data structures used by the
database are not dependent on the format of these files, although the
parsing routines are. Currently, the various formats of these files are:
<ol>
  <li><i><a name="3C1">titles.dbase</a></i>.
  This file contains all of the titles in the database. Each entry
  is separated from the next by a newline character, while each field
  in an entry is separated by the <b>|</b> character. The format of
  each entry (using two-letter codes) is:
  <pre>
  title|AE|YR|SE|PB|SL|<i>extension</i>
  </pre>
  Where <i>extension</i> is one of: 
  <i>n</i> (Novel),
  <i>a</i> (Anthology),
  <i>c</i> (Collection),
  <i>sf</i> (Short Fiction),
  <i>nf</i> (Non Fiction),
  <i>p</i> (Poem),
  <i>ng</i> (Non Genre), or
  <i>ar</i> (Artwork).
  <i>se</i> (Serial).
  <p>

  <li><i><a name="3C2">titles.xba</a></i>.
  This file contains a cross-index to <i>titles.dbase</i>. There is
  one entry per unique author. Each entry consists of the author's list of
  titles. To save space, each title is represented by a hexadecimal offset
  to the entry's location in the <i>titles.dbase</i> file. The format of
  each entry is:
  <pre>
  author|<i>offset_1</i>|<i>offset_2</i>|...|<i>offset_n</i>
  </pre>
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3C3">authors.dbase</a></i>.
  This file contains all of the author data in the database. Each entry
  is separated from the next by a newline character, while each field
  in an entry is separated by the <b>|</b> character. The format of
  each entry (using two-letter codes) is:
  <pre>
  author|LN|BP|BD|DD|PS|
  </pre>
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3C4">awards.dbase</a></i>.
  This file contains all of the awards data in the database. Each entry
  is separated from the next by a newline character, while each field
  in an entry is separated by the <b>|</b> character. The format of
  each entry (using two-letter codes) is:
  <pre>
  title|AE|YR|TP|LV|<i>extension</i>
  </pre>
  Where <i>extension</i> is one of: 
  <i>Ca</i> (Campbell),
  <i>Cl</i> (Clarke),
  <i>Hu</i> (Hugo),
  <i>Lc</i> (Locus),
  <i>Ne</i> (Nebula),
  <i>Pk</i> (P.K Dick),
  <i>St</i> (Bram Stoker), or
  <i>Wf</i> (WFA).
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3C5">awards.xba</a></i>.
  This file contains a cross-index to the <i>awards.dbase</i>. There is
  one entry per unique author. Each entry consists of the author's list of
  awards. To save space, each award is represented by a hexadecimal offset
  to the entry's location in the <i>awards.dbase</i> file. The format of
  each entry is:
  <pre>
  author|<i>offset_1</i>|<i>offset_2</i>|...|<i>offset_n</i>
  </pre>
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3C6">awards.xby</a></i>.
  This file contains a cross-index to the <i>awards.dbase</i>. There is
  one entry per unique year. Each entry consists of the year's list of
  awards. To save space, each award is represented by a hexadecimal offset
  to the entry's location in the <i>awards.dbase</i> file. The format of
  each entry is:
  <pre>
  year|<i>offset_1</i>|<i>offset_2</i>|...|<i>offset_n</i>
  </pre>
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3C7">pubs.dbase</a></i>.
  This file contains all of the publication data in the database. Each entry
  is separated from the next by a newline character, while each field
  in an entry is separated by the <b>|</b> character. The format of
  each entry (using two-letter codes) is:
  <pre>
  AB|title|AE|YR|IS|PB|PR|PP|TP|CV|NT|
  </pre>
  </ol>


<h2><a name="3D">Database Engine Binaries</a></h2>
The ISFDB engine is actually divided into twelve separate binaries. The first
set of binaries are used to accept input from a FORMS-based html page. These
three binaries must be referenced with an http METHOD of POST:
<ol>
  <li><i><a name="3D1">search</a></i>. This binary is capable of performing
  four different types of searches on the database: by author, by title, by
  year, and by series. The <i>search</i> application uses two posted variables:
  <i>arg</i>, and <i>type</i>. The <i>type</i> variable controls the search
  type, and must be set to one of: <i>Name</i>, <i>Title</i>, <i>Series</i>, or
  <i>Year</i>. There are no restrictions on the <i>arg</i> variable.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D2">AwGen</a></i>.  This binary is capable of generating
  an awards page for a specific year.  The <i>AwGen</i> application uses two 
  posted variables: <i>year</i>, and <i>award</i>. The <i>award</i> variable
  controls which award will be listed, and must be set to one of: <i>Hugo</i>, 
  <i>Nebula</i>, <i>WFA</i>, <i>Campbell</i>, <i>Clarke</i>, <i>Locus Poll</i>,
  <i>PK Dick</i>, or <i>Stoker</i>. There are no restrictions on the <i>year</i>
  variable, although years in the later half of the twentieth century are 
  suggested if you want some output.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D3">submit</a></i>. This binary is used to submit new or
  corrected bibliographic information to the database. The
  data is placed in the appropriate file in the <i>dbase.input</i> directory. 
  The <i>submit</i> application uses the following posted variables:
  <i>TITLE</i>, <i>AUTHOR</i>, <i>DATE</i>, <i>SERIES</i>, and
  <i>TYPE</i>. There are no lexical restrictions on the values of these 
  variables, although the data will be tossed unless the following sensible
  values for the <i>TYPE</i> variable are used: 
  <i>Novel</i>, <i>Shortfiction</i>, <i>Nonfiction</i>, <i>Collection</i>,
  <i>Anthology</i>, <i>Artwork</i>, <i>Nongenre</i>, <i>Serial</i>, or <i>Poem</i>.

</ol>
The remaining binaries use an http METHOD of GET:
<ol>
  <li><i><a name="3D4">exact_author</a></i>. This binary outputs an author's
  Summary Bibliography. It takes a single argument, which is the accepted
  author's name. Spaces in the name should be replaced with the <i>_</i>
  character, and single quotes (as in O'Brien) should be backquoted.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D4A">a_exact_author</a></i>. This binary outputs an author's
  Summary Bibliography. It takes a single argument, which is the accepted
  author's name. Spaces in the name should be replaced with the <i>_</i>
  character, and single quotes (as in O'Brien) should be backquoted. Differs
  from exact_author in that the bibliography is displayed in alphabetical
  order.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D5">pbiblio</a></i>.  This binary outputs an entire
  publication bibliography. It takes a single argument, which is the accepted
  author's name.  Spaces and single quotes should be handled as above.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D6">cinfo</a></i>.  This binary outputs a correction
  page.  It takes a single argument, which is the accepted
  author's name.  Spaces and single quotes should be handled as above.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D7">ninfo</a></i>.  This binary outputs a new data
  page form.  It takes a single argument, which is the accepted
  author's name.  Spaces and single quotes should be handled as above.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D8">pwork</a></i>.  This binary outputs the publication
  history for a specific work. It takes a single argument, which is
  the hexadecimal offset to the title entry in the <i>titles.dbase</i>
  file.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D9">plist.cgi</a></i>. This binary outputs the content listing
  of a specific publication. It takes a single argument, which is
  the publication tag associated with the publication.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D10">AwYear</a></i>. This binary outputs a single
  awards page for a specific award. It takes a single argument, in
  Award/Year format: A two-letter award code, followed by a four-digit
  year. The two-letter codes are the same as the two-letter award extensions
  used in the <i>awards.dbase</i> file format definition above.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D11">forthcoming</a></i>. This binary outputs the
  list of forthcoming books. It takes no argument.
  <p>
  <li><i><a name="3D12">cwork</a></i>.  This binary outputs a data correction
  form.  It takes a single argument, which is the hexadecimal offset to the 
  title entry in the <i>titles.dbase</i> file.
</ol>

